course: dictionary
lesson: 6438
title: Nag: keeping on, and a nagging worry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Nag can be a verb or a noun, and it has more than one meaning. The main one is to keep asking or telling someone to do something in an annoying way. «A chore chart lists the household jobs and who does each one, so work is shared fairly and nobody has to nag.» The chart means nobody has to keep telling each other what to do.
explain: Nag can also mean something that keeps worrying or hurting you a little. «He finished faster, because nothing was nagging at the back of his head.» The boy could not stop thinking about something, so this is a worry that keeps coming back. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? A worry that will not go away fits.
explain: Look at the words around nag to find its meaning. Nag is a verb when someone does the nagging, and it is a noun when you talk about a person who nags, or an old horse. Try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right.
fact: Nag can be a verb (nag, nags, nagged, nagging) or a noun (a nag, nags).
fact: To nag means to keep asking or telling someone to do something in an annoying way.
fact: «A nagging feeling» or «a nagging cough» means something that keeps bothering you a little, and does not go away.
fact: Informal: a nag is a person who nags, and a nag can also be an old or weak horse.
fact: A calm, polite request with good reasons is more likely to be listened to than nagging.
exercise: «A chore chart lists the household jobs and who does each one, so nobody has to nag.» What does nag mean in this sentence? | *Keep asking or telling someone to do something in an annoying way | Read a story out loud | Sleep late at the weekend | Draw a picture of the house
exercise: «He finished faster, because nothing was nagging at the back of his head.» What does nagging mean here? | A loud noise from the kitchen | A cheerful song | *A worry that keeps bothering you | A game with a ball
exercise: «The old nag trotted slowly across the field.» What does nag mean in this sentence? | A type of game | A person who keeps asking | *An old or weak horse | A loud shout
test: «A calm, polite request with good reasons is more likely to be listened to than nagging or sneaking.» What does nagging mean in this sentence? | *Keeping on asking in an annoying way | Singing loudly at the table | Asking politely once, with a good reason | Whispering a secret to a friend
test: Ella has a nagging cough that will not go away. What does nagging suggest? | A cough that is loud and sudden | A cough that she really likes | *A cough that keeps coming back and bothers her a little | A cough that has gone for good
exam: Which sentence uses nag as a verb, meaning to keep asking in an annoying way? | She had a nagging doubt about the test | The nag in the story was weak and tired | *Please stop nagging me about the bins, I will do them after tea | The old nag stood quietly by the gate
